---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../components/CaseStudyLayout.astro";

const title = "Vehicle Pairing Wizards — Derek DiLeo";
const description =
  "How I helped turn a technical vehicle-connection workflow into a repeatable, operator-friendly Android experience.";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  eyebrow="Android · Vehicle integration · UX"
  heading="From CLI commands to pairing wizards"
  intro="Turning vehicle pairing into a workflow anyone could use."
>
  <div slot="meta" class="case-study__meta">
    <span>Role</span>
    <strong>Android engineer</strong>
    <span>Scope</span>
    <strong>Pairing workflow</strong>
  </div>

  <div slot="aside">
    <p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p>
    <dl>
      <div><dt>Starting point</dt><dd>CLI + web API</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Primary users</dt><dd>Vehicle operators</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Workflow</dt><dd>Configure → discover → connect</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>Repeatable pairing</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
  <h2>Discovery worked. The workflow didn’t.</h2>
  <p>
    Implementers developed against our SDK and, when a vehicle was discovered,
    the system automatically created a node that became visible and
    controllable in Kinesis. That was a good integration experience for the
    people building plugins, but it left too much work to the operator.
  </p>
  <p>
    Configuring a ground radio through a web API, or worse, a command line, was
    impractical for an everyday user and even less suitable for a soldier
    downrange. We needed a guided way to configure the radio associated with a
    given vehicle platform, discover that vehicle, connect to it, and persist
    the settings for future use.
  </p>

  <p class="eyebrow">The workflow</p>
  <h2>Make the technical path repeatable.</h2>
  <p>
    Shortly after being promoted from junior engineer to L1, I was entrusted
    with substantial autonomy to shape this solution. I had built one Spring
    application by choice in college, but Android was a new domain. Most of my
    deeper learning happened outside coursework; classroom projects often
    stopped at CLI applications. My first eight months at Tomahawk were spent
    inheriting a Redwood.js customer
    portal built with React and TypeScript, then carrying it from infancy
    through production deployment in AWS GovCloud. I then spent roughly six
    months actively contributing to Kinesis while building familiarity with
    its codebase before taking on the pairing work.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first hurdle was not the screen itself. Each pairing wizard was a
    separate Activity defined in the vehicle’s plugin and launched by Kinesis
    through an explicit Intent, so I first had to establish how a plugin could
    advertise that a wizard existed and how Kinesis could launch it on demand.
    We started with one vehicle, where I owned the Android UI and architecture,
    partnered with an integrator who
    handled much of the C++ integration with the vehicle itself, and crossed
    the JNI boundary when the feature required it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once that foundation was working, I drew on established Android patterns,
    including Philipp Lackner’s educational content and public examples, and
    adapted those techniques to our workflows. The first wizard took about
    nine months; after the pattern was established, the next three took only a
    few weeks each.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first pairing wizard turned those disconnected steps into one guided
    flow. A user could select the vehicle type, configure the radio, start
    discovery, review the vehicle that appeared, and complete the connection
    without leaving Kinesis or learning an implementation-specific command.
  </p>
  <p>
    Radios were identified by IP address, which let Kinesis reuse the same
    physical radio across different vehicles. Each vehicle could have its own
    saved configuration—often a different channel. Enabling one of those saved
    configurations kicked off discovery: Kinesis located the radio, applied
    its settings, and attempted to discover the vehicle. Once the vehicle was
    found, the plugin created a node that Kinesis could display and control.
    When a connection needed to be retried, the operator could start the
    process on demand instead of reconstructing the setup from memory or
    repeating manual commands. In practice, that means setting up the kit
    ahead of time, then connecting to it quickly when it is needed.
  </p>

  <p class="eyebrow">The platform model</p>
  <h2>Let plugins describe what they support.</h2>
  <p>
    Kinesis launched vendor plugins at startup, where each plugin began its
    discovery loop and advertised the pairing Activities it supported. Some
    plugins exposed more than one vehicle, each with its own pairing procedure.
    Kinesis compiled those advertisements into a list of available wizards,
    which users could launch when needed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Building that dynamic list was a learning experience of its own. At the
    time, I had not yet worked with Room, was still developing my fluency with
    Kotlin Flows, and had not established Hilt in the main application. I used
    a static registry with an observer-based pub/sub pattern to notify Kinesis
    when new pairing Activities were available. It solved the immediate
    problem, but it remains technical debt—an honest reminder that a working
    first step is not always the final architecture.
  </p>
  <p>
    That model kept vendor-specific pairing logic within each plugin while
    giving the main application a consistent way to discover and present it. The
    result was an extensible integration point without forcing operators to
    understand how each vehicle worked internally.
  </p>

  <p class="eyebrow">What it taught me</p>
  <h2>Good abstractions remove operational friction.</h2>
  <p>
    The SDK solved the technical problem of discovering a vehicle. The wizard
    solved the product problem of helping a person complete the job. That
    distinction has stayed with me: an API can be correct and still leave the
    most important work to the user.
  </p>
  <p>
    I learned a great deal while building the first wizard, and even more when
    I revisited the code later. I found public mutable properties, unnecessary{" "}
    <code>suspend</code> functions, long methods carrying too many
    responsibilities, and domain use cases that did little more than wrap a
    repository. Seeing those patterns clearly today is a reminder of how much
    my engineering judgment has grown. I’m grateful for that perspective: the
    distance between the first implementation and how I would build it today is
    one of the clearest measures of how far I’ve come.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was also fortunate to have senior engineers I could lean on when I got
    stuck. They reinforced that the only silly question is the one you do not
    ask, and taught me that every engineer is still learning—regardless of
    title or years of experience. I carry that lesson forward whenever a junior
    engineer comes to me for help: make the question welcome, work through the
    problem together, and leave them more confident than when they arrived.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pairing wizards also became my first production opportunity to establish
    Jetpack Compose and ViewModel patterns. They gave me a focused workflow in
    which to prove a more modern UI architecture before helping move the wider
    Kinesis application forward. More importantly, they taught me that good
    engineering is iterative: start with a real user problem, establish a
    repeatable pattern, and make each subsequent feature easier to build than
    the last.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .case-study__meta {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, max-content);
    gap: 0.5rem 1.2rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font: 0.68rem/1.3 var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .case-study__meta strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  dl {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
  }
  dl div {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  }
  dt {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font: 0.62rem/1.3 var(--font-mono);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .case-study__meta {
      grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
